1
00:00:00,530 --> 00:00:03,019
During the war, a young codebreaker named

2
00:00:03,019 --> 00:00:05,627
Mavis Lever picked up an intercepted Enigma

3
00:00:05,627 --> 00:00:07,939
message and noticed something strange.

4
00:00:07,939 --> 00:00:09,976
Not a word that was there.

5
00:00:10,163 --> 00:00:11,729
A letter that wasn't.

6
00:00:11,928 --> 00:00:13,765
In the whole message, there was not a

7
00:00:13,765 --> 00:00:14,706
single L.

8
00:00:15,889 --> 00:00:17,981
That absence told her almost exactly what

9
00:00:17,981 --> 00:00:19,290
the message said.

10
00:00:19,290 --> 00:00:21,315
And it points to the strangest weakness of

11
00:00:21,315 --> 00:00:23,058
the machine Germany trusted with its

12
00:00:23,058 --> 00:00:25,807
secrets. To see how a missing letter can be

13
00:00:25,807 --> 00:00:27,966
a clue, we have to open the box.

14
00:00:34,354 --> 00:00:36,670
Enigma looked like a typewriter in a wooden

15
00:00:36,670 --> 00:00:38,775
case. You pressed a key, and instead of

16
00:00:38,775 --> 00:00:40,985
printing, a small lamp lit up a different

17
00:00:40,985 --> 00:00:43,541
letter. One person typed, another wrote

18
00:00:43,541 --> 00:00:45,362
down the glowing letters, and a radio

19
00:00:45,362 --> 00:00:47,728
operator sent the result in Morse code.

20
00:00:48,881 --> 00:00:50,992
Inside, the signal ran through three

21
00:00:50,992 --> 00:00:53,423
rotors, chosen from a box of five.

22
00:00:53,423 --> 00:00:54,901
Each rotor was a wheel with twenty-six

23
00:00:54,901 --> 00:00:56,567
contacts on each face, wired in a scrambled

24
00:00:56,567 --> 00:00:58,842
pattern. And every time a key was pressed,

25
00:00:58,842 --> 00:01:01,472
the right-hand rotor turned one step, like

26
00:01:01,472 --> 00:01:02,775
a car's odometer.

27
00:01:02,775 --> 00:01:03,591
So the same letter came out differently

28
00:01:03,591 --> 00:01:04,276
each time.

29
00:01:04,306 --> 00:01:06,926
Press A five times,

30
00:01:06,997 --> 00:01:10,096
and you might get five different letters.

31
00:01:11,667 --> 00:01:13,932
At the front sat a plugboard, where cables

32
00:01:13,932 --> 00:01:16,145
swapped pairs of letters before and after

33
00:01:16,145 --> 00:01:18,466
the rotors. Count every choice:

34
00:01:18,466 --> 00:01:21,676
which rotors, in what order, their starting

35
00:01:21,676 --> 00:01:24,073
positions, and ten plug cables.

36
00:01:24,073 --> 00:01:26,587
The army version had about one hundred and

37
00:01:26,587 --> 00:01:28,984
fifty-nine quintillion possible starting

38
00:01:28,984 --> 00:01:32,329
settings. And the settings changed every

39
00:01:32,329 --> 00:01:33,113
day.

40
00:01:35,475 --> 00:01:37,227
But the most important part sat at the far

41
00:01:37,227 --> 00:01:39,833
end: a fixed wheel called the reflector.

42
00:01:39,833 --> 00:01:41,173
The current went in through the plugboard,

43
00:01:41,173 --> 00:01:43,720
through all three rotors, hit the

44
00:01:43,720 --> 00:01:44,948
reflector,

45
00:01:45,136 --> 00:01:47,330
and came back out through the rotors by a

46
00:01:47,330 --> 00:01:48,610
different path.

47
00:01:49,756 --> 00:01:51,762
It was a clever convenience.

48
00:01:51,933 --> 00:01:54,096
Because the path works like a mirror, the

49
00:01:54,096 --> 00:01:55,674
machine undoes itself.

50
00:01:55,783 --> 00:01:58,711
If A became G, then with the same settings,

51
00:01:58,711 --> 00:01:59,714
G became A.

52
00:01:59,937 --> 00:02:01,818
The receiver needed no separate decoding

53
00:02:01,818 --> 00:02:03,001
mode. Set the same settings, type the

54
00:02:03,001 --> 00:02:06,042
scrambled text, and the original message

55
00:02:06,042 --> 00:02:07,438
lights up.

56
00:02:08,813 --> 00:02:10,838
But a mirror has a blind spot.

57
00:02:10,931 --> 00:02:12,825
The current could never come back out on

58
00:02:12,825 --> 00:02:14,389
the wire it went in on.

59
00:02:14,409 --> 00:02:16,739
So no letter could ever be encrypted as

60
00:02:16,739 --> 00:02:19,087
itself. A is never A.

61
00:02:19,353 --> 00:02:20,886
It sounds like a tiny detail.

62
00:02:20,886 --> 00:02:23,555
Yet it meant Enigma's output was not truly

63
00:02:23,555 --> 00:02:26,651
random. It leaked one fact, every single

64
00:02:26,651 --> 00:02:27,385
time.

65
00:02:29,599 --> 00:02:31,444
The first people to break Enigma were not

66
00:02:31,444 --> 00:02:33,874
British. In December 1932, a

67
00:02:33,874 --> 00:02:36,023
twenty-seven-year-old mathematician named

68
00:02:36,023 --> 00:02:37,969
Marian Rejewski, working for Poland's

69
00:02:37,969 --> 00:02:40,254
Cipher Bureau, attacked it with permutation

70
00:02:40,254 --> 00:02:42,712
theory, the mathematics of shuffles.

71
00:02:43,866 --> 00:02:46,012
French intelligence passed him documents

72
00:02:46,012 --> 00:02:48,346
from a German spy, Hans-Thilo Schmidt,

73
00:02:48,346 --> 00:02:50,043
including some daily settings.

74
00:02:50,043 --> 00:02:52,396
Combining them with clever equations,

75
00:02:52,396 --> 00:02:54,462
Rejewski worked out the internal wiring of

76
00:02:54,462 --> 00:02:56,479
the rotors without ever seeing a military

77
00:02:56,479 --> 00:03:00,208
machine. With his colleagues Jerzy Różycki

78
00:03:00,208 --> 00:03:02,670
and Henryk Zygalski, the Bureau read Enigma

79
00:03:02,670 --> 00:03:05,216
traffic for years, using card catalogues,

80
00:03:05,216 --> 00:03:07,588
perforated sheets, and machines they called

81
00:03:07,588 --> 00:03:08,455
bombas.

82
00:03:10,181 --> 00:03:11,938
Then Germany made it harder.

83
00:03:11,938 --> 00:03:14,573
In December 1938, two extra rotors raised

84
00:03:14,573 --> 00:03:16,957
the possible rotor orders from six to

85
00:03:16,957 --> 00:03:19,628
sixty. Poland lacked the resources to keep

86
00:03:19,628 --> 00:03:22,392
up. So in July 1939, five weeks before the

87
00:03:22,392 --> 00:03:24,732
war began, the Poles handed their methods,

88
00:03:24,732 --> 00:03:28,384
and copies of the machine, to the British

89
00:03:28,384 --> 00:03:29,447
and the French.

90
00:03:31,698 --> 00:03:33,965
The work moved to Bletchley Park, a country

91
00:03:33,965 --> 00:03:35,881
house northwest of London, where thousands

92
00:03:35,881 --> 00:03:38,238
of people would eventually work in secret.

93
00:03:38,252 --> 00:03:40,319
Among them was a young mathematician named

94
00:03:40,319 --> 00:03:41,383
Alan Turing.

95
00:03:41,437 --> 00:03:43,601
No machine could check quintillions of

96
00:03:43,601 --> 00:03:45,212
settings one by one.

97
00:03:45,212 --> 00:03:47,281
The codebreakers needed a shortcut.

98
00:03:47,281 --> 00:03:49,847
They found it in human habits.

99
00:03:51,262 --> 00:03:53,741
German messages were full of routine.

100
00:03:53,741 --> 00:03:54,869
Weather reports.

101
00:03:54,869 --> 00:03:57,458
Standard phrases, like 'keine besonderen

102
00:03:57,458 --> 00:03:59,822
Ereignisse': nothing to report.

103
00:03:59,834 --> 00:04:01,922
If you could guess a phrase that probably

104
00:04:01,922 --> 00:04:04,290
appeared in a message, you had a crib, a

105
00:04:04,290 --> 00:04:06,133
piece of the original text.

106
00:04:06,133 --> 00:04:08,649
The only question was where it sat.

107
00:04:10,004 --> 00:04:12,472
This is where the blind spot pays off.

108
00:04:12,472 --> 00:04:14,458
Slide the guessed phrase along the

109
00:04:14,458 --> 00:04:15,808
scrambled text.

110
00:04:15,839 --> 00:04:18,009
Wherever any letter of the guess lands on

111
00:04:18,009 --> 00:04:20,273
the same letter in the ciphertext, that

112
00:04:20,273 --> 00:04:22,579
position is impossible, because no letter

113
00:04:22,579 --> 00:04:24,164
can encrypt to itself.

114
00:04:24,244 --> 00:04:26,714
Positions fall away one after another,

115
00:04:26,714 --> 00:04:28,727
until only a few remain.

116
00:04:30,967 --> 00:04:33,265
A crib in the right place gives a chain of

117
00:04:33,265 --> 00:04:35,872
letter pairs: this letter became that one,

118
00:04:35,872 --> 00:04:37,051
at this step.

119
00:04:37,331 --> 00:04:39,656
Turing realised the pairs could be linked

120
00:04:39,656 --> 00:04:41,162
into loops, and that a wrong rotor setting

121
00:04:41,162 --> 00:04:41,813
would make those loops contradict

122
00:04:41,813 --> 00:04:44,796
themselves. So he designed a machine to

123
00:04:44,796 --> 00:04:48,900
hunt for settings without contradictions:

124
00:04:48,970 --> 00:04:49,980
the Bombe.

125
00:04:51,422 --> 00:04:52,944
Gordon Welchman added a crucial

126
00:04:52,944 --> 00:04:55,806
improvement, the diagonal board, which used

127
00:04:55,806 --> 00:04:57,378
another German convenience:

128
00:04:57,378 --> 00:04:59,970
plugboard swaps work both ways.

129
00:05:00,350 --> 00:05:03,003
The first Bombe, named Victory, arrived at

130
00:05:03,003 --> 00:05:05,196
Bletchley Park in March 1940.

131
00:05:05,231 --> 00:05:07,905
Many more followed, run around the clock,

132
00:05:07,905 --> 00:05:10,041
largely by women of the Women's Royal Naval

133
00:05:10,041 --> 00:05:10,913
Service.

134
00:05:12,385 --> 00:05:14,111
Each Bombe chattered through rotor

135
00:05:14,111 --> 00:05:16,035
positions, rejecting the impossible at

136
00:05:16,035 --> 00:05:17,365
electrical speed.

137
00:05:17,454 --> 00:05:19,336
When it stopped, it offered a candidate

138
00:05:19,336 --> 00:05:22,072
setting. Codebreakers then tested it on a

139
00:05:22,072 --> 00:05:23,275
copy of Enigma.

140
00:05:23,435 --> 00:05:25,706
If German words appeared, the day's key for

141
00:05:25,706 --> 00:05:27,851
that network was broken, and its messages

142
00:05:27,851 --> 00:05:28,924
could be read.

143
00:05:31,034 --> 00:05:33,315
Which brings us back to Mavis Lever and the

144
00:05:33,315 --> 00:05:34,757
message with no L.

145
00:05:34,930 --> 00:05:36,994
She worked in Dilly Knox's team, on the

146
00:05:36,994 --> 00:05:39,142
Enigma used by the Italian Navy.

147
00:05:39,357 --> 00:05:41,512
That operator had been told to send a dummy

148
00:05:41,512 --> 00:05:43,902
message. As she later recalled, he simply

149
00:05:43,902 --> 00:05:46,475
pressed the last key on the keyboard, L,

150
00:05:46,475 --> 00:05:48,089
over and over.

151
00:05:49,443 --> 00:05:50,857
Every letter of the ciphertext could be

152
00:05:50,857 --> 00:05:51,715
anything except L.

153
00:05:51,715 --> 00:05:54,915
So the one missing letter gave the whole

154
00:05:54,915 --> 00:05:57,880
message away: L, L, L, L, all the way

155
00:05:57,880 --> 00:06:01,498
through. She called it the biggest crib

156
00:06:01,498 --> 00:06:04,507
they ever had, and it revealed the wiring

157
00:06:04,507 --> 00:06:05,772
of a new rotor.

158
00:06:05,812 --> 00:06:08,233
One lazy minute, plus one design

159
00:06:08,233 --> 00:06:10,926
convenience, opened the machine.

160
00:06:13,309 --> 00:06:15,623
Historians still debate exactly how much

161
00:06:15,623 --> 00:06:17,938
these intercepts changed the war, though

162
00:06:17,938 --> 00:06:20,365
many argue the intelligence known as Ultra

163
00:06:20,365 --> 00:06:21,589
shortened it.

164
00:06:21,589 --> 00:06:24,486
What is clear is how Enigma fell.

165
00:06:24,609 --> 00:06:26,953
Not by brute force alone, but through its

166
00:06:26,953 --> 00:06:29,284
structure: a reflector that made one

167
00:06:29,284 --> 00:06:32,216
outcome impossible, and people who repeated

168
00:06:32,216 --> 00:06:33,318
themselves.

169
00:06:34,838 --> 00:06:37,501
In 1883, the cryptographer Auguste

170
00:06:37,501 --> 00:06:39,650
Kerckhoffs wrote a rule that security

171
00:06:39,650 --> 00:06:41,402
engineers still follow:

172
00:06:41,402 --> 00:06:43,956
a cipher should stay safe even if the enemy

173
00:06:43,956 --> 00:06:46,465
knows exactly how the machine works.

174
00:06:46,465 --> 00:06:48,536
Only the key should be secret.

175
00:06:48,617 --> 00:06:51,124
Enigma's designers trusted the key.

176
00:06:51,124 --> 00:06:53,266
But the machine's own structure leaked

177
00:06:53,266 --> 00:06:56,112
information, whatever the key was.

178
00:06:57,663 --> 00:07:00,072
In a well-designed modern cipher, such as

179
00:07:00,072 --> 00:07:02,976
AES, the output looks like random noise.

180
00:07:02,976 --> 00:07:04,974
Any symbol can turn into any other,

181
00:07:04,974 --> 00:07:06,294
including itself.

182
00:07:06,360 --> 00:07:09,139
That is the quiet lesson of Enigma.

183
00:07:09,139 --> 00:07:11,524
A pattern doesn't have to be something that

184
00:07:11,524 --> 00:07:14,618
appears. Sometimes, the clue is what can

185
00:07:14,618 --> 00:07:15,979
never appear.

186
00:07:18,202 --> 00:07:19,968
So the next time something looks perfectly

187
00:07:19,968 --> 00:07:22,697
random, ask what it never does.

188
00:07:23,014 --> 00:07:25,677
Latoon. Voyaging the unseen.

